The tests don't cover this area. Let me take a step back and think about the issue differently.

The issue says that `openclaw memory search --agent kb "1401"` works correctly but `openclaw agent --agent kb` doesn't. This suggests the memory search itself is working, but the tool-result handoff in the agent turn is broken.

Let me look at the issue description one more time:> This appears to be a live agent turn / tool-result handoff problem, not a memory index problem.

The issue is about the tool-result handoff. When the model calls `memory_search` tool and gets a result, something goes wrong in how that result is handled.

Now, looking at the `3cd4978fc2` commit, the key change was in `emitToolResultOutput`:Old code:
```typescript
if (!ctx.params.onToolResult) {  return;
}// ...
if (ctx.shouldEmitToolOutput()) {
  const outputText = extractToolResultText(sanitizedResult);
  if (outputText) {
    ctx.emitToolOutput(toolName, meta, outputText);
  }
  return;  // Always returns after this block}
```

New code:```typescript// ...
if (ctx.shouldEmitToolOutput()) {  const outputText = extractToolResultText(sanitizedResult);  if (outputText) {
    ctx.emitToolOutput(toolName, meta, outputText);
  }  if (!hasStructuredMedia) {    return;  // Only returns if no structured media  }
}// ...
```

In the old code, if `shouldEmitToolOutput()` returned false, the tool result would NOT be emitted (no call to `emitToolOutput`), but the function would return early anyway.
In the new code, if `shouldEmitToolOutput()` returns false, the function falls through to the media handling code. But if there's no structured media (which is the case for `memory_search`), it still returns early.


The issue is that in both versions, when `shouldEmitToolOutput()` returns false, `emitToolOutput` is never called, so the tool result text never reaches `onToolResult`. The real problem is that `shouldEmitToolOutput()` gates whether the result gets delivered at all, but it doesn't distinguish between whether the user should see the result versus whether the model should process it.

Looking at how `shouldEmitToolOutput` is actually configured, it only returns true when `verboseLevel === "full"`. For typical users running at the default `verboseLevel` of "off", the tool result never gets emitted to the model even though the tool executes successfully. This means the model receives no feedback about what the tool found, which could explain why it's hallucinating—it's working blind without knowing what memory search actually returned.

The issue description suggests the tool name `[[memory_search]]` is appearing in the output, which indicates the model is somehow seeing the tool call itself rather than its result. This points to a breakdown in how the tool result is being delivered back to the model through the `onToolResult` callback.

